### The bearer-token API (/api/**), called the way a Flutter app, a SPA or another service calls it.
#
# Setup once: docker compose up -d, ./docker/logto/setup.sh (creates the API resource, its
# permissions, the "api-demo-client" machine-to-machine app, and http/http-client.private.env.json),
# then start the app (port 8101). In IntelliJ pick the "local" environment and run top to bottom.
#
# This token is for an APPLICATION (client credentials, no user). A Flutter app gets a token for a
# USER instead (authorization code + PKCE through the Logto SDK, with the same "resource"); the API
# checks it exactly the same way, only the permissions come from the user's roles.

### 1. Get an access token for the API resource from Logto (client credentials)
POST {{logto}}/oidc/token
Authorization: Basic {{clientId}} {{clientSecret}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&resource={{apiResource}}&scope=read:reports write:reports

> {%
    client.test("token issued", () => client.assert(response.status === 200, response.body));
    const token = response.body.access_token;
    client.global.set("token", token);
    // Only the permissions the client's roles grant: read:reports, not write:reports.
    client.log("granted scope: " + response.body.scope);
    // For step 7: one character changed inside the signature. (Appending a character would not do:
    // an extra trailing base64url character carries no whole byte and is ignored when decoding.)
    const i = token.length - 10;
    client.global.set("tamperedToken", token.slice(0, i) + (token[i] === "A" ? "B" : "A") + token.slice(i + 1));
%}

### 2. Who am I? Any valid token: subject + client_id = the app, audience = this API
GET {{app}}/api/whoami
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {% client.test("200", () => client.assert(response.status === 200)); %}

### 3. Read reports: needs read:reports -> 200
GET {{app}}/api/reports
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {% client.test("200", () => client.assert(response.status === 200)); %}

### 4. Create a report: needs write:reports, which this client does not have -> 403
POST {{app}}/api/reports
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"name": "Q3"}

> {% client.test("403", () => client.assert(response.status === 403)); %}

### 5. No token -> 401 with WWW-Authenticate (never a redirect to a sign-in page)
GET {{app}}/api/reports

> {% client.test("401", () => client.assert(response.status === 401)); %}

### 6a. A valid, signed Logto token for ANOTHER resource (the Management API; it carries no permissions)
POST {{logto}}/oidc/token
Authorization: Basic {{clientId}} {{clientSecret}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&resource=https://default.logto.app/api

> {% client.global.set("otherToken", response.body.access_token); %}

### 6b. ... is refused by this API -> 401: its "aud" is not http://localhost:8101/api
GET {{app}}/api/whoami
Authorization: Bearer {{otherToken}}

> {% client.test("401", () => client.assert(response.status === 401)); %}

### 7. A tampered token -> 401 "Invalid signature": it no longer matches Logto's key
GET {{app}}/api/whoami
Authorization: Bearer {{tamperedToken}}

> {% client.test("401", () => client.assert(response.status === 401)); %}


### ================================================================================================
### As a signed-in USER: what a Flutter app does (authorization code + PKCE, public client, no secret)
#
# {{$auth.token("logto-user")}} uses the "logto-user" OAuth2 config that setup.sh wrote into
# http-client.private.env.json (IntelliJ 2024.1+). On the first request IntelliJ opens Logto's sign-in
# page in its built-in browser; sign in (or create an account: the code arrives at
# http://localhost:8026). The token is cached and refreshed by the IDE.
#
# Permissions come from the user's Logto roles: new users get "user" (read). For write, give yourself
# "admin": LOGTO_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com ./docker/logto/setup.sh, then sign in again (in IntelliJ:
# the token's context menu / "Clear" in the Auth tool window) so a new token carries the permission.

### 8. Who am I? subject = the Logto user id, clientId = api-tester
GET {{app}}/api/whoami
Authorization: Bearer {{$auth.token("logto-user")}}

### 9. Read reports: role "user" or "admin" -> 200
GET {{app}}/api/reports
Authorization: Bearer {{$auth.token("logto-user")}}

### 10. Create a report: role "admin" -> 201, role "user" -> 403
POST {{app}}/api/reports
Authorization: Bearer {{$auth.token("logto-user")}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"name": "Created by a user"}
